1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:15,848
On the eighth of January 1959, a bearded thirty-two-year-old lawyer rode into Havana at the head of a column of rebels, cheered by enormous crowds. The dictator he had fought had fled a week earlier.

2
00:00:16,948 --> 00:00:21,919
Fidel Castro promised Cubans freedom, honest government and elections.

3
00:00:23,019 --> 00:00:29,461
He went on to rule Cuba for forty-nine years. In all that time, he never faced a free election.

4
00:00:30,561 --> 00:00:38,156
More than a million Cubans left the island, many risking their lives at sea. Thousands of his opponents were imprisoned.

5
00:00:39,256 --> 00:00:54,659
To his admirers he was a hero who gave the poor schools and doctors, and stood up to the United States. To his critics, above all the Cubans who fled, he was a dictator who jailed his own people. Both views have evidence behind them.

6
00:00:56,859 --> 00:01:09,083
Fidel Castro was born on the thirteenth of August, 1926, in Birán, in eastern Cuba. His father was a wealthy sugar planter who had come from Spain.

7
00:01:10,183 --> 00:01:22,620
He was sent to elite Jesuit schools, excelled at sport, and studied law at the University of Havana, where he threw himself into the rough, often violent world of student politics.

8
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:39,272
In 1952, months before an election, a former army sergeant turned general, Fulgencio Batista, seized power in a coup. Castro had been a candidate for parliament. He decided the answer was armed revolt.

9
00:01:41,472 --> 00:01:56,555
On the twenty-sixth of July 1953, he led more than a hundred young men in an attack on the Moncada army barracks in Santiago de Cuba. It failed badly. Many of the rebels who were captured were killed.

10
00:01:57,655 --> 00:02:04,268
At his trial he defended himself in a long speech that ended with words that became famous.

11
00:02:05,368 --> 00:02:13,347
He was sentenced to fifteen years, but was freed in an amnesty after less than two. He went into exile in Mexico.

12
00:02:14,447 --> 00:02:21,209
There he met Ernesto Guevara, a young Argentine doctor known as Che, who joined his movement.

13
00:02:22,309 --> 00:02:38,011
In December 1956, eighty-two rebels crossed to Cuba on an overloaded yacht called the Granma. Batista's army was waiting. Only a small band survived and escaped into the Sierra Maestra mountains.

14
00:02:39,111 --> 00:02:49,329
From there they fought a guerrilla war. Batista's brutality and corruption drove more and more Cubans to support them, and the rebels grew into an army.

15
00:02:50,429 --> 00:02:57,491
On New Year's Day 1959, Batista fled the country. The revolution had won.

16
00:02:59,691 --> 00:03:06,112
Castro became prime minister. His charisma was enormous, and he could hold a crowd for hours.

17
00:03:07,212 --> 00:03:17,367
Within months, revolutionary tribunals had tried and executed hundreds of Batista's police and soldiers, many after hasty trials.

18
00:03:18,467 --> 00:03:26,232
He broke up large estates, slashed rents and seized American-owned businesses. The promised elections never came.

19
00:03:27,332 --> 00:03:42,607
In 1961 he sent hundreds of thousands of volunteers, many of them teenagers, into the countryside to teach reading. By the end of that year, Cuba reported that illiteracy had fallen to about four percent.

20
00:03:44,807 --> 00:04:00,871
The United States cut trade with Cuba and, in April 1961, backed an invasion by about fourteen hundred Cuban exiles trained by the CIA at the Bay of Pigs. Castro's forces crushed it within three days.

21
00:04:01,971 --> 00:04:22,664
Castro declared his revolution socialist and allied with the Soviet Union. In October 1962, the discovery of Soviet nuclear missiles in Cuba set President Kennedy against the Soviet leader, Nikita Khrushchev, and brought the world closer to nuclear war than ever before or since.

22
00:04:23,764 --> 00:04:36,692
The CIA, a US Senate committee later found, plotted repeatedly to kill him, at one point with help from the Mafia. He survived them all, and held power through ten American presidencies.

23
00:04:37,792 --> 00:04:52,384
Che Guevara left Cuba to spread revolution abroad. In 1967 he was captured by the Bolivian army, with CIA help, and executed. He became an icon; Castro remained in power.

24
00:04:54,584 --> 00:05:06,531
The price was freedom. Cuba became a one-party communist state. The press, radio and television were controlled by the state, and independent parties and unions were banned.

25
00:05:07,631 --> 00:05:19,300
From 1960, Committees for the Defence of the Revolution were set up on almost every street, to organise residents and report signs of disloyalty.

26
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:39,131
Over the decades, thousands of political prisoners passed through his jails. In the 1960s, gay men, religious believers and others judged unfit were sent to forced labour camps. Late in life, Castro called this a great injustice, and said he was responsible.

27
00:05:40,231 --> 00:05:48,721
Some prisoners of conscience spent twenty years or more behind bars. Human rights groups were not allowed to inspect the prisons.

28
00:05:49,821 --> 00:06:01,235
In 1980, he suddenly let anyone who wanted to leave depart from the port of Mariel. About a hundred and twenty-five thousand people left in six months.

29
00:06:02,335 --> 00:06:11,743
Over the decades more than a million Cubans emigrated, most to Florida, where they built a powerful exile community that never forgave him.

30
00:06:13,943 --> 00:06:29,836
Castro also exported revolution. Cuban troops fought in Africa, above all in Angola, where they faced the army of apartheid South Africa. Nelson Mandela later thanked Cuba for its part in the struggle against apartheid.

31
00:06:30,936 --> 00:06:44,291
At home, healthcare and education were free. Cuba trained huge numbers of doctors and sent them to work across the developing world, and its life expectancy came to match that of far richer countries.

32
00:06:45,391 --> 00:07:01,625
But the economy depended on Soviet subsidies. When the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991, Cuba's economy shrank by about a third. Cubans call it the Special Period: years of hunger, blackouts and bicycles.

33
00:07:02,725 --> 00:07:12,325
In 1994, after rare riots in Havana, tens of thousands of Cubans set out for Florida on rafts and inner tubes.

34
00:07:14,525 --> 00:07:27,880
In July 2006, after emergency intestinal surgery, Castro handed power temporarily to his younger brother, Raúl. In 2008, the handover became permanent.

35
00:07:28,980 --> 00:07:42,633
In retirement he wrote newspaper columns and received visiting leaders, often in a tracksuit. He lived to see the United States and Cuba restore diplomatic relations in 2015.

36
00:07:43,733 --> 00:07:50,219
Fidel Castro died on the twenty-fifth of November 2016, aged ninety.

37
00:07:51,319 --> 00:07:58,615
In Havana, crowds queued for days to pay their respects. In Miami, exiles celebrated in the streets.

38
00:08:00,815 --> 00:08:12,484
His admirers, especially across Latin America and Africa, remember a leader who gave the poor dignity, and a small island that stood up to a superpower for half a century.

39
00:08:13,584 --> 00:08:23,376
His critics remember the firing squads, the prisons and the families split by the Florida Strait, and an economy that left Cubans poor and dependent.

40
00:08:24,476 --> 00:08:36,679
As of 2025, the system he built survives, but Cuba is suffering severe shortages and blackouts, and hundreds of thousands of Cubans have left in recent years.

41
00:08:37,779 --> 00:08:48,061
Castro's revolution made real promises to the poor, and kept some of them. The price was that Cubans were never allowed to vote on whether it was worth it.

42
00:08:49,161 --> 00:08:59,743
His ashes lie inside a large granite boulder in the cemetery of Santa Ifigenia, in Santiago de Cuba, the city where his revolution began.
